<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rohingya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rohingya people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rohingya are a mainly Muslim people of Rakhine State in western Myanmar, close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. Myanmar's 1982 citizenship law left most of them stateless, and they have faced repeated persecution. In 2017 army 'clearance operations' drove over 700,000 of them into Bangladesh, where they live in large refugee camps, and the violence has been examined by international courts as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rohingya.jpg" alt="Rohingya" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rohingya Language Academy SVG versionː MS Sakib · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rohingya_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Rohingya are an Indo-Aryan ethnolinguistic group from Rakhine State, historically Arakan, a coastal region on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> between South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. By the 4th century Arakan was one of the earliest Indianised kingdoms in Southeast Asia. Arab merchants are recorded on the coast of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from the 9th century, and the Rohingya trace their history to this period. Early evidence of Bengali Muslim settlement in Arakan dates from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/min-saw-mon">Min Saw Mon</a> (1430–34) of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mrauk-u">Mrauk U</a>, who regained his throne in 1430 with military help from the Bengal Sultanate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Rohingya maintain that they are indigenous to western Myanmar, with a heritage of over a millennium; the Myanmar government regards them as migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> in the colonial and postcolonial periods. After the Konbaung Dynasty conquered Arakan in 1785, as many as 35,000 people fled to the Chittagong region in 1799. Under British rule, thousands of Bengalis from Chittagong settled in Arakan in the early 19th century as farm labourers. A separate administrative zone for the Rohingya-majority north, the Mayu Frontier District, existed from 1961 to 1964.</p><p>After the coup of 1962 the Rohingya were increasingly deprived of political rights. A military operation in 1978 sent an estimated 200,000 refugees into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, most of whom later returned. A citizenship law of 1982 did not list the Rohingya among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>'s 135 national races, which left much of the population stateless. Further crackdowns followed in 1991–1992 and 2012, and from 2016 most Rohingya in Myanmar were driven into Bangladesh. The International Criminal Court began an investigation of crimes against humanity, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> heard a case alleging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Before that crackdown about 1.4 million Rohingya lived in Myanmar, chiefly in the northernmost townships of Rakhine, where they formed 80 to 98 per cent of the people. Their language is Indo-Aryan and related to Chittagonian, and both are related to Bengali but are not mutually intelligible with it. It has been written in Arabic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, Roman and Burmese scripts and in Hanifi, a newer alphabet derived from Arabic. Most Rohingya are Muslim, following a blend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Christian minorities. Myanmar's governments have rejected the name Rohingya and call the community Bengali.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rohingya people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state recognises 135 &quot;national races&quot; but under the citizenship law of 1982 excludes most <b>Rohingya</b> Muslims of Rakhine State.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
